          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em pseudo-equivalence relation
            is like an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+relation"} equivalence relation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+graph"} directed graph
            of the relation is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+pseudograph"} directed pseudograph
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magmoids"} magmoids
            are the raw structure used to build
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semicategories"} semicategories
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dagger+categories"} dagger categories
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoids"} groupoids
            (i.e. a groupoid without associativity, unital laws, and inverse laws).
          %p
            Sets equipped with pseudo-equivalence relations are sometimes called
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/setoids"} setoids
            (i.e. in
            = succeed ";" do
              %a{:href => "#Wilander12"} Wilander (2012), §4
            = succeed ";" do
              %a{:href => "#PalmgrenWilander14"} Palmgren &amp; Wilander (2014), §2, §6
            = succeed ";" do
              %a{:href => "#EmmeneggerPalmgren20"} Emmenegger &amp; Palmgren (2020), §6
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#Cipriano20"} Cipriano (2020), Def. 1.1.1
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+relations"} equivalence relations
          %h2#definition Definition
