                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t:E \to V
            are
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/jointly+monic"} jointly monic
          %p
            A setoid is
            %strong thin
            or
            %strong simple
            if its underlying loop directed pseudograph is a loop digraph. In both cases, the pseudo-equivalence relation becomes an
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+relation"} equivalence relation
          %p
            A thin setoid is equivalently a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/thin+category"} thin
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dagger+category"} dagger category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category"} enriched
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/truth+values"} truth values
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
          %p Sometimes in the mathematical literature, setoids are thin by default.
          %h2#core_of_a_pseudoequivalence_relation Core of a pseudo-equivalence relation
          %p
            For any pseudo-equivalence relation
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/core"} core
